WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.275 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When a multiplier has two digits, does the tens digit count
as ones?

00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:08.248 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are multiplying by two-digit numbers.

00:00:08.468 --> 00:00:14.902 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will separate tens and ones, calculate two partial
products, and connect them to the complete multiplication.

00:00:15.552 --> 00:00:20.264 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to multiply by two-digit numbers using
tens and ones.

00:00:20.914 --> 00:00:22.673 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:22.893 --> 00:00:25.964 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can split a multiplier into tens and ones.

00:00:26.184 --> 00:00:29.360 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can label and calculate both partial products.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.379 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can add the products and check their size.

00:00:33.029 --> 00:00:35.455 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Thirteen means ten plus three.

00:00:35.675 --> 00:00:40.170 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Multiplying by thirteen means taking ten groups and three
more groups.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:45.488 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Missing the tens value is a common error; label each partial
product before adding.

00:00:45.708 --> 00:00:49.649 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In a small picture, imagine two rows of thirteen counters.

00:00:49.869 --> 00:00:52.240 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each row has ten and three more.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.015 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both parts still belong in the answer.

00:00:55.665 --> 00:00:59.038 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Calculate two hundred and forty-six times thirteen.

00:00:59.258 --> 00:01:05.099 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First take ten groups: two hundred and forty-six times ten
is two thousand four hundred and sixty.

00:01:05.749 --> 00:01:11.190 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then take three groups: two hundred and forty-six times
three is seven hundred and thirty-eight.

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:15.677 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Add the partial products to get three thousand one hundred
and ninety-eight.

00:01:16.327 --> 00:01:20.018 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now calculate three hundred and fifteen times twenty-four.

00:01:20.238 --> 00:01:22.537 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Twenty-four is twenty plus four.

00:01:22.757 --> 00:01:26.948 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Three hundred and fifteen times twenty is six thousand three
hundred.

00:01:27.598 --> 00:01:30.807 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four groups make one thousand two hundred and sixty.

00:01:31.027 --> 00:01:36.038 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Combining twenty groups and four groups gives seven thousand
five hundred and sixty.

00:01:36.688 --> 00:01:39.769 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The two in twenty-four means twenty, not two.

00:01:39.989 --> 00:01:43.870 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Multiplying by two instead of twenty misses a place value.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:48.955 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Label twenty groups and four groups, then combine the
correct partial products.

00:01:49.605 --> 00:01:52.312 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What is one hundred and two times twelve?

00:01:56.312 --> 00:02:01.035 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Ten groups make one thousand and twenty, and two groups make
two hundred and four.

00:02:01.255 --> 00:02:04.472 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The total is one thousand two hundred and twenty-four.

00:02:05.122 --> 00:02:08.196 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Split the multiplier into tens and ones.

00:02:08.416 --> 00:02:15.620 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Calculate both partial products with their correct place
values, add them, then check with a nearby friendly
multiplication.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.143 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a model and explain why your strategy works.

00:02:19.363 --> 00:02:22.475 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the lesson practice on MathMagic!
